ple DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:spec] :writes [:draft-code]}] [:llm {:reads [:draft-code :spec] :writes [:critique]}] [:llm {:reads [:draft-code :critique] :writes [:refined-code]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** code-building without an explicit :spec input (just a goal string) produces code that maps to the model's first guess at what the goal meant — divergence from intent surfaces only at test time (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the goal is ambiguous OR the consequences of wrong-interpretation are expensive\n    - Recommended fix: always pipe code-building's :reads through a design stage first; the design's :spec field is the input to code-building, not the raw goal\n\n  - **Failure mode:** single-shot generation of a long file (>200 lines) produces code with internal inconsistencies — function signatures mismatch their callers, imports declared but unused (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the artifact is a long file with multiple cohesive sections\n    - Recommended fix: scaffold via :code-built outline (top-of-file imports / function-declarations / glue), then :map-each per-section generation, then :code-stitched final — preserves consistency across sections\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - function implementation from a typed spec + examples\n  - config file generation from a deployment plan\n  - test case generation from a behavior specification\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general sh